A fixed volume control leaves the samples alone.

`--volume-ctrl fixed` reads as "do not touch the audio", and that is how
the app uses it: the supervisor owns this device's volume, sends it to
the speakers through the engine, and wants librespot's samples at full
scale. In 0.8 the flag does not do that. Fixed is not one of the mapped
controls, so to_mapped falls through to the linear arm and the volume
lands in the soft mixer as a plain fraction, and get_soft_volume hands
the player a live SoftVolume whatever the control is. Every sample was
multiplied by the remote's volume.

So each volume change was heard twice. The speakers applied the level
the supervisor sent them, at once, and then the samples that had been
scaled by the same level reached them about three seconds later, once
the pipe and the engine's buffer had drained. A drag to half volume
played as half, then a quarter.

Now get_soft_volume returns NoOpVolume for a fixed control, which is
what a hardware mixer hands the player, and the mixer goes on tracking
the volume so the remotes still see it. Every other control keeps its
SoftVolume.

Applies on top of librespot-control-socket.patch,
librespot-session-loss-reconnect.patch,
librespot-play-from-stopped.patch,
librespot-pause-keeps-position.patch and
librespot-volume-events-only-from-remotes.patch, none of which touch
the mixer.

